    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm reviewing our HACCP plan for a large event next week. Can you give me the specific temperature and time requirements for the cooling stage of cooked poultry? I want to make sure we're fully covering the risks associated with Clostridium perfringens.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, according to the USDA's Food Safety and Inspection Service, you need to cool cooked poultry from 137°F (57°C) to 70°F (21°C) within 2 hours and then from 70°F (21°C) to 40°F (4°C) within an additional 4 hours. Ensuring this rapid temperature reduction helps prevent the growth of Clostridium perfringens, which thrives in the \"danger zone\" between 40°F and 140°F (4°C and 60°C). Double-check that your cooling method aligns with these guidelines before the event.",
      "source": "target",
