    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Our kitchen is quite busy so we often have large batches sitting in deep containers. What are the best practices for cooling those down safely so we don't run into any issues with uneven temperatures or bacterial growth in the center of the batch?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For large batches of cooked poultry, it is essential to implement the \"two-stage cooling\" approach. You'll need to use shallow, wide containers that serve as cooling trays to spread the poultry out, promoting more rapid and even cooling. To maintain consistent temperatures and avoid bacterial growth, stir or turn the poultry periodically during the cooling process, especially during the second stage. This ensures that all parts of the batch reach 70°F within 2 hours and all reach 40°F within the next 4 hours as required. Also, monitor the temperatures at multiple points using a food thermometer to guarantee even cooling throughout the batch.",
